WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000 align:start position:0% line:0%
Transcriber: Leslie Gauthier
Reviewer: 

00:00:01.036 --> 00:00:04.476
The wheel was invented a long time ago,

00:00:04.476 --> 00:00:05.636
but we’re way behind.

00:00:06.916 --> 00:00:09.236
Hi, my name is Jane Velkovski.

00:00:09.796 --> 00:00:11.796
I’m 13 years old,

00:00:11.796 --> 00:00:13.796
a student in a public school

00:00:13.796 --> 00:00:15.516
and a passionate football fan.

00:00:16.316 --> 00:00:17.796
In addition to that,

00:00:17.796 --> 00:00:19.796
I’m a kid with a disability.

00:00:20.516 --> 00:00:23.756
My condition is known as SMA:

00:00:23.756 --> 00:00:25.956
spinal muscular atrophy,

00:00:25.956 --> 00:00:30.356
where the muscles are getting weak
but the brain remains strong.

00:00:31.916 --> 00:00:32.916
I love football.

00:00:33.276 --> 00:00:35.196
I'm a passionate football fan,

00:00:35.196 --> 00:00:38.636
and my passion
has brought me so much joy

00:00:38.636 --> 00:00:40.876
and so many great experiences --

00:00:40.876 --> 00:00:44.316
so far, more than I could
have ever imagined.

00:00:45.436 --> 00:00:51.196
For example, I was offered to join
a brilliant campaign by UEFA,

00:00:51.196 --> 00:00:55.276
that is the Union of European
Football Associations,

00:00:55.276 --> 00:00:58.996
to raise awareness about equality
and accesss issues.

00:00:59.596 --> 00:01:00.596
It meant,

00:01:00.596 --> 00:01:02.196
among other things,

00:01:02.196 --> 00:01:06.196
I got the honor of public recognition,

00:01:06.196 --> 00:01:07.796
public space

00:01:07.796 --> 00:01:09.996
and I got an opportunity

00:01:09.996 --> 00:01:13.076
to meet several
famous players who are my heroes.

00:01:14.476 --> 00:01:16.596
I have also had the opportunity

00:01:16.596 --> 00:01:21.436
to speak at the General Assembly 
of the United Nations in New York

00:01:21.436 --> 00:01:25.876
as part of the World Children’s Day
celebration in 2019.

00:01:27.396 --> 00:01:30.236
These are just a few
of the amazing moments

00:01:30.236 --> 00:01:31.316
I’ve been able to do.

00:01:32.356 --> 00:01:35.036
But the thing is,

00:01:35.036 --> 00:01:37.196
it’s not just a coincidence

00:01:37.196 --> 00:01:39.556
or simple luck that this happened to me.

00:01:40.156 --> 00:01:44.316
I was able to do all of this
not because it was easy

00:01:44.316 --> 00:01:46.396
or simple to perform,

00:01:46.396 --> 00:01:50.916
but because some important things
were given to me,

00:01:50.916 --> 00:01:54.116
like the support of my family, of course,

00:01:54.116 --> 00:01:56.596
inclusive education

00:01:56.596 --> 00:01:59.236
and the environment I was raised in --

00:01:59.236 --> 00:02:03.316
but also very much
because of my wheelchair.

00:02:04.196 --> 00:02:07.076
It literally has made
all of this possible.

00:02:08.196 --> 00:02:10.036
I’m independent with my chair.

00:02:10.756 --> 00:02:13.916
I’m free to go wherever I want to.

00:02:14.676 --> 00:02:17.636
I’m able to do things
I couldn’t otherwise.

00:02:19.076 --> 00:02:24.116
My motorized wheelchair gives me three
incredibly important things:

00:02:24.116 --> 00:02:25.756
freedom,

00:02:25.756 --> 00:02:27.356
independence

00:02:27.356 --> 00:02:28.356
and ability.

00:02:30.116 --> 00:02:31.756
Some people ask,

00:02:31.756 --> 00:02:33.356
“isn’t it just a wheelchair?”

00:02:33.876 --> 00:02:35.556
It seems like a small thing,

00:02:35.556 --> 00:02:36.556
no big deal.

00:02:37.036 --> 00:02:38.036
But it’s not.

00:02:38.356 --> 00:02:39.596
It’s a big deal to me.

00:02:40.236 --> 00:02:42.476
It's what made all of this possible.

00:02:44.076 --> 00:02:46.116
But the question is:

00:02:46.116 --> 00:02:48.956
Do you think every kid like me
has a wheelchair?

00:02:49.556 --> 00:02:50.556
No.

00:02:50.996 --> 00:02:52.716
I did a little research

00:02:52.716 --> 00:02:54.796
and I found out that today,

00:02:54.796 --> 00:02:59.156
over one billion people require
assistive technology

00:02:59.156 --> 00:03:01.796
to achieve their full potential,

00:03:01.796 --> 00:03:04.716
but 90 percent do not have the access

00:03:04.716 --> 00:03:07.476
to the assistive technology
that they need.

00:03:08.396 --> 00:03:12.236
That means only one in 10 people
with disabilities,

00:03:12.236 --> 00:03:15.036
who are in need of assistive technology,

00:03:15.036 --> 00:03:16.036
have them.

00:03:17.476 --> 00:03:19.036
I’m that one in 10;

00:03:19.036 --> 00:03:20.036
I’m the lucky one.

00:03:21.316 --> 00:03:23.716
But still in my country,

00:03:23.716 --> 00:03:28.436
I’m not eligible to get a power chair
until I’m in school,

00:03:28.436 --> 00:03:30.476
which means around six years old.

00:03:31.116 --> 00:03:33.476
So from two to six years old,

00:03:33.476 --> 00:03:36.916
how am I supposed to move
myself around independently?

00:03:37.676 --> 00:03:40.236
I’m supposed to be pushed
by another person

00:03:40.236 --> 00:03:41.756
when I want to get out of bed

00:03:41.756 --> 00:03:44.476
or go outside my home
before the age of six?

00:03:46.076 --> 00:03:47.756
Do you think this is OK?

00:03:48.796 --> 00:03:50.356
No, not at all.

00:03:50.876 --> 00:03:53.676
So let me tell you how I got my chair.

00:03:55.076 --> 00:03:58.036
I was around two
when I started to wheel around.

00:03:58.756 --> 00:04:01.276
Every day, out with my neighbors,

00:04:01.276 --> 00:04:05.556
but first using my manual wheelchair
pushed by my mom --

00:04:05.556 --> 00:04:08.876
which, to be honest, kind of makes it hard

00:04:08.876 --> 00:04:12.076
to be just a kid hanging out
with his friends

00:04:12.076 --> 00:04:14.036
when your mom was always right there.

00:04:15.276 --> 00:04:19.276
My parents had been looking for ways
to get me a power wheelchair

00:04:19.276 --> 00:04:21.836
a long time before school,

00:04:21.836 --> 00:04:24.476
but it took them years to finally have it.

00:04:25.116 --> 00:04:26.756
When I was five years old,

00:04:26.756 --> 00:04:29.396
a year before I started primary school,

00:04:29.396 --> 00:04:30.996
I got this power chair.

00:04:31.716 --> 00:04:35.316
And it was not from the government
or health insurance

00:04:35.316 --> 00:04:38.796
or even because my parents
could afford it,

00:04:38.796 --> 00:04:44.116
but because it was a donation 
from another family overseas,

00:04:44.116 --> 00:04:49.236
from a child with SMA who had outgrown
the chair in the United States.

00:04:50.676 --> 00:04:52.956
It was a gift in every sense.

00:04:53.556 --> 00:04:57.596
Their donation gave me
the freedom I needed so, so much.

00:04:58.236 --> 00:05:03.076
Now my mom says I have a better social
life than all her friends and herself.

00:05:04.596 --> 00:05:06.796
When I was a very small kid,

00:05:06.796 --> 00:05:11.396
my parents, like others,
were usually speaking on my behalf

00:05:11.396 --> 00:05:13.276
to the people in our surroundings.

00:05:13.996 --> 00:05:15.516
Even to the kid down there,

00:05:15.516 --> 00:05:18.356
answering questions about my chair,

00:05:18.356 --> 00:05:19.436
my legs,

00:05:19.436 --> 00:05:20.436
my condition --

00:05:20.436 --> 00:05:21.436
all of that.

00:05:21.636 --> 00:05:24.476
They were saying, 
“Oh yeah, sure, he can play.

00:05:24.876 --> 00:05:26.036
His legs are a bit weak,

00:05:26.036 --> 00:05:29.116
but he can do the same things as you

00:05:29.116 --> 00:05:30.396
only in a different way.”

00:05:31.556 --> 00:05:35.156
I think that pretty much gave me
an idea of myself

00:05:35.156 --> 00:05:37.036
and how to live my life

00:05:37.036 --> 00:05:38.716
and what to say to the world.

00:05:39.796 --> 00:05:42.836
That was the beginning
of my wonderful social life.

00:05:43.916 --> 00:05:45.836
Later, in kindergarten,

00:05:45.836 --> 00:05:49.116
I was able to drive my power chair freely

00:05:49.116 --> 00:05:50.116
and enjoy life.

00:05:50.796 --> 00:05:51.956
By the age of 10,

00:05:51.956 --> 00:05:54.396
I knew how to keep myself safe,

00:05:54.396 --> 00:05:58.036
so I just needed a bit of assistance
for transfers and books.

00:05:58.636 --> 00:06:01.516
Otherwise, I was independent

00:06:01.516 --> 00:06:04.436
and so happy going everywhere
with my friends.

00:06:05.316 --> 00:06:09.116
I have wonderful friends
from my school and my neighborhood.

00:06:09.956 --> 00:06:12.156
I really enjoy spending time with them.

00:06:13.836 --> 00:06:16.116
But can you imagine

00:06:16.116 --> 00:06:19.596
how my childhood would look like
without my power chair?

00:06:20.596 --> 00:06:21.596
I cannot.

00:06:22.716 --> 00:06:28.316
I’m forever grateful to the family
that donated my power chair,

00:06:28.316 --> 00:06:31.036
but my freedom

00:06:31.036 --> 00:06:32.596
and independence

00:06:32.596 --> 00:06:37.116
and ability should not depend on luck

00:06:37.116 --> 00:06:38.236
or charity.

00:06:38.676 --> 00:06:39.916
No one’s should be.

00:06:40.396 --> 00:06:43.556
This assistive product
needs to be available

00:06:43.556 --> 00:06:46.076
to everyone who needs it.

00:06:47.756 --> 00:06:49.396
By the age of two,

00:06:49.396 --> 00:06:51.996
most children are able
to walk on their own.

00:06:52.876 --> 00:06:57.436
Those children who have
some sort of disability and can’t walk,

00:06:57.436 --> 00:06:59.876
they should be given an AT,

00:06:59.876 --> 00:07:02.036
an assistive technology,

00:07:02.036 --> 00:07:03.316
in order to move.

00:07:03.836 --> 00:07:07.316
If they have a proper wheelchair
from an early age,

00:07:07.316 --> 00:07:12.436
they will have a great opportunity
to discover the world around them.

00:07:13.956 --> 00:07:15.476
Freedom of movement,

00:07:15.476 --> 00:07:18.756
no matter on legs or on wheels,

00:07:18.756 --> 00:07:20.356
is a human right,

00:07:20.356 --> 00:07:26.876
which means policymakers have to provide
what children with disabilities need

00:07:26.876 --> 00:07:28.476
in order to move.

00:07:29.556 --> 00:07:32.876
It’s about being able
to go to the park to play,

00:07:32.876 --> 00:07:34.196
go to school,

00:07:34.196 --> 00:07:35.476
go to work --

00:07:35.476 --> 00:07:37.356
really, everything.

00:07:37.796 --> 00:07:39.076
This chair is my legs.

00:07:39.716 --> 00:07:41.756
This chair is my life.

00:07:43.596 --> 00:07:45.836
The wheel was invented a long time ago.

00:07:46.956 --> 00:07:48.596
And thanks to that invention,

00:07:48.596 --> 00:07:49.956
I can move my stuff around.

00:07:50.676 --> 00:07:54.796
So this time, literally, the wheel
doesn’t need to be reinvented.

00:07:55.356 --> 00:07:56.876
We just need policies

00:07:56.876 --> 00:08:01.876
to make sure that wheelchairs
are available to everyone in need.

00:08:03.556 --> 00:08:05.476
But it’s not only just the wheelchair.

00:08:06.036 --> 00:08:08.156
We also need physical infrastructure.

00:08:08.476 --> 00:08:09.956
For example ramps,

00:08:09.956 --> 00:08:10.956
elevators,

00:08:10.956 --> 00:08:12.596
roads without obstacles,

00:08:12.596 --> 00:08:14.556
accessible transportation,

00:08:14.556 --> 00:08:16.356
school buses,

00:08:16.356 --> 00:08:19.756
so our environment becomes
more accessible for all.

00:08:21.276 --> 00:08:24.636
We live in a society
that’s not giving enough support

00:08:24.636 --> 00:08:29.276
to fulfill the potential
of young people with disability,

00:08:29.276 --> 00:08:30.316
like me.

00:08:31.196 --> 00:08:34.996
We need to think about
how to change that at a global level.

00:08:36.276 --> 00:08:38.236
If society removes the barriers

00:08:38.236 --> 00:08:41.556
that prevent me
from doing everyday things,

00:08:41.556 --> 00:08:44.156
I can become a very productive person

00:08:44.156 --> 00:08:45.796
and give back to the society.

00:08:46.796 --> 00:08:51.276
I have so much to contribute
of my intellect,

00:08:51.276 --> 00:08:52.276
my brain.

00:08:53.316 --> 00:08:56.596
I believe I can become
a lawyer or an architect,

00:08:56.596 --> 00:08:57.716
whatever I want to.

00:08:58.396 --> 00:09:01.396
Also, I’m an excellent football coach

00:09:01.396 --> 00:09:03.036
and a strategist,

00:09:03.036 --> 00:09:04.996
so I have that to contribute, too.

00:09:05.796 --> 00:09:10.436
I have so many things I can do
to help my community,

00:09:10.436 --> 00:09:11.636
my country

00:09:11.636 --> 00:09:12.716
and the world.

00:09:13.676 --> 00:09:16.356
We may think of that as a mutual benefit.

00:09:16.836 --> 00:09:18.076
“How?” you may ask.

00:09:19.036 --> 00:09:20.996
Let me tell you how I see it.

00:09:23.196 --> 00:09:27.436
An able body doesn’t give you
a direct pass to a happy life.

00:09:28.196 --> 00:09:32.796
A person able to walk
still needs help with other things --

00:09:32.796 --> 00:09:34.276
things that I can do well.

00:09:35.076 --> 00:09:36.516
So for example,

00:09:36.516 --> 00:09:41.876
for me, helping a friend on a math test
is the same as him helping me

00:09:41.876 --> 00:09:42.876
with my backpack.

00:09:43.356 --> 00:09:44.556
I don’t see a difference.

00:09:44.716 --> 00:09:46.596
He’s struggling, I’m struggling,

00:09:46.596 --> 00:09:48.436
he’s good with his stronger hands,

00:09:48.436 --> 00:09:50.156
I’m good with my strong knowledge;

00:09:50.156 --> 00:09:51.356
we’re helping each other.

00:09:51.676 --> 00:09:53.516
That’s what friends do,

00:09:53.516 --> 00:09:55.356
and that’s what societies should do.

00:09:57.876 --> 00:10:00.436
Once I was out with my friends

00:10:00.436 --> 00:10:02.836
and an old man approached me

00:10:02.836 --> 00:10:05.716
and started a very nice
and polite conversation.

00:10:07.116 --> 00:10:11.436
By simply asking me, first,
“What is your condition?”

00:10:11.436 --> 00:10:16.516
I felt deeply touched by the honesty
and simplicity of his question.

00:10:17.276 --> 00:10:18.716
I was only 11

00:10:18.716 --> 00:10:22.236
and felt so respected that he asked me

00:10:22.236 --> 00:10:25.676
and felt very comfortable
to speak on my behalf

00:10:25.676 --> 00:10:26.716
and answer him.

00:10:27.676 --> 00:10:30.836
I explained to him why I can’t walk

00:10:30.836 --> 00:10:33.196
and what I need in order to function.

00:10:34.156 --> 00:10:36.516
Usually people only stare.

00:10:37.036 --> 00:10:40.476
Yeah, yeah, yeah, I know it's not
a standard picture of a child.

00:10:41.676 --> 00:10:43.316
My wheelchair is very interesting.

00:10:43.716 --> 00:10:45.716
I was even cuter when I was small.

00:10:46.236 --> 00:10:48.396
But still,

00:10:48.396 --> 00:10:50.756
it’s not comfortable when people stare.

00:10:51.556 --> 00:10:54.156
It’s better not to stare, but to care.

00:10:55.156 --> 00:10:56.996
If you see a wheelchair user,

00:10:56.996 --> 00:11:00.916
rather than staring
and wondering about them,

00:11:00.916 --> 00:11:01.916
look around

00:11:01.916 --> 00:11:05.236
and see if there’s something
you can do to help.

00:11:06.196 --> 00:11:07.956
If there aren’t ramps,

00:11:07.956 --> 00:11:10.156
if doorways are too narrow,

00:11:10.156 --> 00:11:14.796
maybe that’s something you could work
to get fixed in your building,

00:11:14.796 --> 00:11:15.996
in your city

00:11:15.996 --> 00:11:17.156
or in your country.

00:11:18.836 --> 00:11:21.596
Each one of us can make a small change,

00:11:21.596 --> 00:11:25.076
but governments
are supposed to make great changes.

00:11:26.356 --> 00:11:31.116
I, and people like me, need help
from public policymakers

00:11:31.116 --> 00:11:34.556
to be able to live
with freedom and independence.

00:11:35.476 --> 00:11:39.916
I wish that more people
were like that old man,

00:11:39.916 --> 00:11:44.556
asking me, and children
like me, what we need

00:11:44.556 --> 00:11:46.756
and listening to our answers.

00:11:48.636 --> 00:11:51.036
I told you at the beginning,
I love football.

00:11:51.716 --> 00:11:53.636
Just in case I wasn’t clear,

00:11:53.636 --> 00:11:56.476
I really love football, a lot.

00:11:57.156 --> 00:12:00.636
And to me, the whole world
is like a giant football team.

00:12:01.956 --> 00:12:07.836
But I took part in the campaign #EqualGame
not only because I love the game

00:12:07.836 --> 00:12:09.996
but because I strongly believe

00:12:09.996 --> 00:12:14.196
that everyone can play
all around the globe:

00:12:14.196 --> 00:12:16.036
men, women,

00:12:16.036 --> 00:12:17.396
abled, disabled,

00:12:17.396 --> 00:12:18.756
young, old --

00:12:18.756 --> 00:12:19.756
doesn’t matter.

00:12:20.116 --> 00:12:25.236
All of them were able to find their way
to play the game that they love.

00:12:25.996 --> 00:12:29.036
All they needed
was the barriers to be removed.

00:12:30.756 --> 00:12:33.316
So I see this world as a playground

00:12:33.316 --> 00:12:36.476
where people and government
are like a team,

00:12:36.476 --> 00:12:41.196
and we need to make sure
that everyone is able to play.

00:12:42.396 --> 00:12:43.396
Thank you.

